411 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/T (The living conditions for dairy animals are frequently overcrowded and unhygienic, with poor-quality feed and inconsistent access to veterinary care, further diminishing their well-being. Calves are routinely separated from their mothers at birth, and male calves are neglected or abandoned. Spent cattle are also rampantly abandoned. These practices directly contravene the provisions of the Prevention of Cruelty to Animals Act, 1960.)
/E (The living conditions for dairy animals are frequently overcrowded and unhygienic, with poor-quality feed and inconsistent access to veterinary care, further diminishing their well-being. Calves are routinely separated from their mothers at birth, and male calves are neglected or abandoned. Spent cattle are also rampantly abandoned. These practices directly contravene the provisions of the Prevention of Cruelty to Animals Act, 1960.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [0 1]
>>
endobj
412 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/T (Moreover, the growing intensification of dairy farming places increasing strain on land, water, and air resources. Manure runoff pollutes surface and groundwater, while antibiotic residues enter the environment through effluents. Greenhouse gas emissions from enteric fermentation further contribute to climate change. These environmental impacts are compounded by serious public health risks such as zoonotic diseases, antimicrobial resistance, and chemical residues in milk, which remain largely unaddressed in most subsidy schemes.)
/E (Moreover, the growing intensification of dairy farming places increasing strain on land, water, and air resources. Manure runoff pollutes surface and groundwater, while antibiotic residues enter the environment through effluents. Greenhouse gas emissions from enteric fermentation further contribute to climate change. These environmental impacts are compounded by serious public health risks such as zoonotic diseases, antimicrobial resistance, and chemical residues in milk, which remain largely unaddressed in most subsidy schemes.)
/Pg 11 0 R
>>
endobj
